referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int branchNotebookPeakTicket(TreeNode root, int k) {
    TreeNode a=root; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {class Walk {void visit(TreeNode node){if(node==null)return;out.add(node.val);visit(node.left);visit(node.right);}}new Walk().visit(a);}
    int answer=out.isEmpty()?0:out.get(0);for(int x:out)answer=Math.max(answer,x);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

A binary tree is a set of nodes, each with a value and up to two children: left and right. root is the top node. Visit a node by recording its value, then visit its whole left branch, then its whole right branch. Use this same rule at every node. Skip missing children. The result list is the recorded values. An empty tree gives an empty result list.

Return the answer

Return the largest number in the result list. If the list is empty, return 0. If all numbers are negative, return the largest negative number; do not replace it with 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the number of nodes. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

